@h1 Images & figures h1@

You place, size, align, and caption an image with plain writer-friendly
parameters — no CSS. Use the image node for a bare image, and the figure node
with a caption node for a captioned one. The sizing and alignment you ask for
compile to self-contained inline styles, so they travel intact into HTML, raw
output, and PDF alike.

@h2 A bare image h2@

An image needs a source and, for accessibility, alternative text:

@@@pre
@img |src ./lamp.png| |alt The keeper's lamp at dusk|
pre@@@

The source is a path or URL; the alternative text describes the image for anyone
who cannot see it. Keep the alternative text on a single parameter — an
apostrophe inside it is fine.

@h2 A captioned figure h2@

Wrap an image in a figure and add a caption node to pair a picture with its
label:

@@@pre
@figure
@img |src ./chart.svg| |size large|
@figcaption Figure 1. Downloads by platform, this cycle. figcaption@
figure@
pre@@@

The figure centres itself and its caption by default, so a figure drops into a
column and looks right with no further fuss.

@h2 Sizing h2@

The size parameter takes a named preset, a pixel number, or a percentage. The
presets and pixel values _cap_ the width (they never enlarge a small source); a
percentage or the full keyword _fills_ the available width:

@table |rows [[You write, Emitted style, Behaviour], [size small, max-width 220px, caps the width], [size medium, max-width 380px, caps the width], [size large, max-width 600px, caps the width], [size full, width 100%, fills the column], [size 240, max-width 240px, caps at that many pixels], [size 50%, width 50%, fills that fraction]]|

So a preset guarantees an image never balloons past a comfortable width, while
the full keyword or a percentage lets a chart stretch to the column. If a source
image is smaller than the cap, it stays its natural size — presets only shrink.

@h2 Alignment h2@

The align parameter takes left, center, or right — but it means slightly
different things on an image versus a figure:

@ul
@li _On an image_, align block-positions the image itself: centred, pushed right,
or pushed left within its line. li@
@li _On a figure_, align sets the alignment of the figure's _contents_ — the image
and its caption together. li@
ul@

Size and alignment compose. An image asked for medium and centred emits both the
width cap and the centring margins in one style attribute:

@@@pre
@img |src ./map.png| |alt Coast survey| |size medium| |align center|
pre@@@

A figure given a size caps its box and auto-centres it, which is why captioned
figures sit centred on the page without your asking.

@h2 Paths, and images in PDF h2@

Keep image paths relative to your document. When you build a PDF, the build
renders through a headless browser from a temporary file and injects a base URL
into the page that points back at your document's own directory — so a relative
path like @code ./assets/chart.svg code@ still resolves. HTML output resolves
paths relative to wherever the finished HTML file sits. Either way, relative
paths are the safe habit.

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li _Expecting a preset to enlarge a small image._ Presets and pixel values only
cap the width. To fill the column, use the full keyword or a percentage. li@
@li _Splitting alternative text across parameters._ Alternative text is one
parameter; keep the whole description on a single one. li@
@li _Using absolute paths that break in PDF._ Prefer relative paths; the PDF
base URL is built to resolve them. li@
ul@

@aside(class note) 💡 A captioned figure wraps its caption in a paragraph inside
the figure. It is a harmless cosmetic detail of the output — your caption still
renders correctly and centred. aside@

